<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Booker T. Washington</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1915">1915</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Booker Taliaferro Washington</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Booker T. Washington was an American educator, author and orator, from 1890 to 1915 the foremost leader of the African-American community. Born into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in Hale's Ford, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, he was freed when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> troops reached the area during the Civil War, worked his way through the Hampton Institute and in 1881 became the first leader of the Tuskegee Institute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>. His Atlanta Address of 1895 brought him national prominence. He promoted self-help, education and Black business, helping to found the National Negro Business League, and his autobiography Up from Slavery appeared in 1901.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/booker-t-washington.jpg" alt="Booker T. Washington" width="330" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harris &amp; Ewing · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Booker_T_Washington_retouched_flattened-crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
